WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:06.920
This is the opening scene of season two of
Broad City. Abbi and llana, the show’s creators

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:11.929
and best friend protagonists are just trying
to survive a chaotic subway ride.

00:00:11.929 --> 00:00:13.049
“Val? Val!”

00:00:13.049 --> 00:00:18.430
And the camera kinda lands on these other
two women who are standing there with pepper

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:19.590
sprayed eyes.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:20.610
That's Caroline Framke

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:26.840
I write about culture at Vox. I’ve watched
and analyzed this show to death.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:32.340
Like many of the best moments in Broad City,
the entire scene is super surreal, so the

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.379
two women probably wouldn’t stand out to
a first time viewer.

00:00:35.379 --> 00:00:41.859
But, if you watch Broad City religiously like
we do, you’ll immediately get the joke.

00:00:41.859 --> 00:00:46.999
Those two women are a callback to a moment
in the first season when Abbi and Ilana actually

00:00:46.999 --> 00:00:51.359
got pepper sprayed. They’re wearing the
same clothes and have the same devastated

00:00:51.359 --> 00:00:59.469
looking faces. Ilana’s doppelganger is even
holding the same curtain rod.

00:00:59.469 --> 00:01:06.710
That’s a callback.

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:12.710
Your typical joke in a TV comedy requires
very little space between the setup. Take

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:14.430
the classic Dick Van Dyke Show

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:14.770
Rob: “Honey will you grab me a handkerchief?”
Laura: “We’re only going next door!”

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:14.850
[ VOX CITY LOGO ]

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.950
Your typical joke in a TV comedy requires
very little space between the setup.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.180
[CLIP] Dick Van Dyke Show:
Rob: “Honey will you grab me a handkerchief?”

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:21.850
Laura: “We’re only going next door!”

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:22.680
and the punchline.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:26.140
Rob: “Well I guess I’ll run back here
to blow my nose.”

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:29.829
If you’ve never seen the show before you’re
still guaranteed a good laugh.

00:01:29.829 --> 00:01:32.700
[Audience laughter]
But there’s another set of much more intricate

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.759
jokes that are written specifically for diehard
viewers.

00:01:36.759 --> 00:01:40.090
They are callbacks, running gags, and easter
eggs.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:43.270
So, what’s a callback exactly?

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:49.009
A callback references a moment or joke that
most likely happened in a previous episode.

00:01:49.009 --> 00:01:53.140
Seinfeld was one of the first shows to employ
callbacks pretty regularly.

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:59.729
To use two famous Seinfeld episodes. One to
set it up is “The Contest” Where all the

00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:02.249
characters compete to see who could go the
longest without masturbating.

00:02:02.249 --> 00:02:05.969
“So you’re still master of your domain?”
“Yes yes I am.”

00:02:05.969 --> 00:02:10.450
Caroline: George wins. Then later in the “Puffy
Shirt” episode -

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:11.690
That’s Season 5

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:14.860
George is trying to be a hand model.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:19.849
A client makes a reference to a previous client
being "Master of his domain."

00:02:19.849 --> 00:02:24.129
Client: "He was not master of his domain."

00:02:24.129 --> 00:02:25.470
And George says not to worry about it

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:29.550
George "You don't have to worry about me.
I won a contest."

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:33.530
If you’re a casual viewer you’re not gonna
know what that means.

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:38.099
But if you’re a viewer that’s stuck with
Seinfeld a little bit you know exactly what

00:02:38.099 --> 00:02:39.260
that means.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:44.409
Callbacks can very easily turn into running
gags if they keep happening again and again.

00:02:44.409 --> 00:02:46.620
Like, no one knows what a chicken sounds like
on Arrested Development.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:50.340
That is not how a chicken sounds. Chickens
don't CLAP!

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:50.890
coo ca coo coo ca coo

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:53.970
chi cha chi cha chi cha
Awe this is priceless. Coo ca coo

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:57.330
A coo da doodle doo a coo da doodle doo

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:01.099
You're a chicken! coo coo ca chaaw coo coo
ca chaw!

00:03:01.099 --> 00:03:06.750
You're the chicken, not me. Clock clock clock
clock claaaawwwww

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:10.230
Or how Kenneth the page is an immortal flesh
vessel in 30 Rock.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:12.760
Kenneth: I've worn this old jacket since 19
hub a dah

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:17.909
You killed the bird I had for almost 60 years.
Sure I was legally dead for 5 minutes

00:03:17.909 --> 00:03:20.900
Who said I've been alive forever?
Because when I look into a mirror there's

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.180
just a white haze.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:23.629
And in Broad City?

00:03:23.629 --> 00:03:27.180
Abbi always having to clean increasingly gross
things out of the gyms bathroom.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:28.799
Trey: Got something for ya.
Abbi: Oh okay

00:03:28.799 --> 00:03:30.709
Trey: Big ol clogged toilet in the women's
room.

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:32.709
Trey: We got another pube situation in the
women's locker room

00:03:32.709 --> 00:03:35.409
Trey: We could really use some Abbi magic
there's a pube situation in the locker room

00:03:35.409 --> 00:03:36.560
that is unprecedented.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.709
Trey: Somebody barfed on an excercise ball
and then in bounced down 2 flights of stairs.

00:03:40.709 --> 00:03:40.939
So.

00:03:40.939 --> 00:03:45.569
Trey: There's a bloody shit spew in the spin
studio. But really there's a bloddy shit spill

00:03:45.569 --> 00:03:46.069
in the spin studio.

00:03:47.349 --> 00:03:54.670
A good example of how intertwined callbacks
and running jokes can get is a stylistic choice

00:03:54.670 --> 00:04:00.219
that Broad City has made for a couple of moments.
When Abbi and Ilana are thrown off or disoriented

00:04:00.219 --> 00:04:01.959
by a piece of news.

00:04:01.959 --> 00:04:06.860
Morgan: “Oh my God, she doesn’t know?”
Ilana: “Know what?”

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:14.560
And it’s because Ilana finds out that Abbi
made out with a friend of theirs.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:20.910
Morgan: “Darcy and Abbi made out!”

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:24.340
Ilana has always wanted to make out with Abbi
and has never gotten to, so this is a very

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:26.220
personal blow to her.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:31.530
This is a direct callback, shot for shot,
to a moment when Abbi misses a package delivery

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:43.639
that’s she’s supposed get for her neighbor
/ crush, Jeremy.

00:04:43.639 --> 00:04:54.060
At this point? That’s a callback. If they
use it again and keep using it, that can become

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:55.169
a running joke.

00:04:55.169 --> 00:04:59.750
One of my favorite callbacks in Broad City
actually begins in “Working Girls” when

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:02.030
Abbi has to track down Jeremy’s lost package.

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:05.830
Operator: "Your borough's distribution center
is on North Brother Island.

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:08.639
Abbi: "Is that in New York? I've never even
heard of that."

00:05:08.639 --> 00:05:10.210
Operator: "Yeah it's in the East River"

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:14.560
Here we're introduced to Garol.

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:21.000
Abbi: "Hello, Garol. I'm not sure I'm in the
right place."

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.030
Garol:" You are."

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:25.860
Garol was the keeper of lost packages.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:30.919
In other words an old lady eating yogurt alone
in a warehouse on North Brother Island.

00:05:30.919 --> 00:05:37.620
You would think Garol is just an amazingly
cast one hit wonder, but fast forward 12 episodes

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:43.180
and she’s the patron of a speakeasy that
Abbi performs at when she’s blackout drunk.

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:55.050
Abbi performing as this sort of Liza Minelli-ish
lounge singer with this very exaggerated accent

00:05:55.050 --> 00:06:01.949
is so wonderful because if you are a diehard
Broad City fan you might remember what we’ll

00:06:01.949 --> 00:06:03.300
call an easter egg.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.710
Abbi: Val about town. Town about Val.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:08.660
Crowd: Hey Val! Val!

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:14.569
Ilana: “How did I not know that when Abbi
gets blackout drunk she becomes Val?”

00:06:14.569 --> 00:06:20.490
Wait, Val? Let’s rewind back to the 2nd
season premiere.

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:24.660
“Val? Val!”

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:30.900
The best part about this entire moment is
how Abbi is just as confused as we are. At

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:33.430
that point we’d never heard of Val before.

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:36.759
It's a very elaborate set up.

00:06:36.759 --> 00:06:39.090
It's an amazing easter egg.

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:44.360
Almost every viewer will get a running gag.
More attentive viewers will get callbacks.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:45.860
Only diehards will get easter eggs.

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:50.960
If a TV show can pull off Callbacks, running
gags, and easter eggs it can really build

00:06:50.960 --> 00:07:28.909
out a much more satisfying world.

